{0 osx-keychain}

Typed OCaml bindings to the macOS Keychain (the [SecItem*] API) for storing and
retrieving passwords — a structured alternative to shelling out to
[/usr/bin/security].

- {b Structured results.} Real [OSStatus] codes and a typed [result]; "not
  found" is [Ok None], not a string you have to grep for.
- {b Exact binary round-trip.} Secrets with embedded NULs / high bytes survive
  intact (the [security] CLI hex-dumps them).
- {b No subprocess.} No process spawn per call; secrets don't pass through argv,
  stdout, the process table, or shell history.
- {b Tiny dependency footprint.} Hand-written C stubs over [Security.framework];
  no [ctypes], no CoreFoundation binding — just [dune].

{1 API reference}

The entry point is the {!module:Osx_keychain} module: {!module:Osx_keychain.Generic_password}
for service/account secrets and {!module:Osx_keychain.Internet_password} for
server-keyed credentials.

{1 Scope}

v1 targets the {b file-based keychain} (the login keychain reached by the
[security] CLI). It works on {b unsigned binaries with no entitlements} — the
common case for CLI tools and daemons.

The data-protection keychain, Touch ID / [SecAccessControl], and iCloud sync are
out of scope: they require an Apple Developer provisioning profile both to use
and to test. A [Data_protection] backend parameter exists but is experimental and
unverified.

macOS only.

